<v Ira Glass>第二幕，戴夫的憎恨。戴夫和鲍勃曾经是最好的朋友。现在戴夫开始使用成人在政治局势、权力斗争中使用的所有策略，那种让人们反目成仇的手段。耳语、类似的事情。鲍勃，顺便说一句，他现在是纽约市的一名戏剧导演，他讲述了这个故事。

00:20:55.720 --> 00:21:35.000 line:0%
<v Bob>I remember going into class in seventh grade on the first day of school and him not talking to me and everybody just wondering. The whole school was wondering why there was this big fight going on between Dave and Bob. And I remember not having an answer for anybody. I used to tell them to ask Dave. I had no idea. And I used to tell people that he just stopped talking to me. And I remember that he became completely bent on turning everyone against me. The whole school. And he did it very well because he's so organized and because he's so manipulative.

00:20:55.720 --> 00:21:35.000 line:80%
<v Bob>我记得七年级开学第一天走进教室，他不跟我说话，每个人都在纳闷。全校都在纳闷为什么戴夫和鲍勃之间有这么大的冲突。我记得对任何人都没有答案。我过去常告诉他们去问戴夫。我根本不知道。我告诉人们他只是停止跟我说话了。我记得他完全一心想要让所有人反对我。全校。他做得非常好，因为他非常有组织性，而且他非常擅长操纵。

00:21:35.000 --> 00:22:02.420 line:0%
<v Bob>And I saw him. And I saw him telling people to say things to me. And he was one who formed the I Hate-- they used to call me [? Cooze ?] then. And he was the one who formed the I Hate [? Cooze ?] Club. He never did anything to me himself. He would never come up to me and make fun of me. He would never come up to me and hit me. He would never assault me personally. But he would just get everyone else to do it for him.

00:21:35.000 --> 00:22:02.420 line:80%
<v Bob>我看着他。我看着他让别人说那些话来针对我。是他组建了“我恨[？]俱乐部”。他自己从来没有对我做过什么。他从不走过来嘲笑我。他从不走过来打我。他从未对我进行过人身攻击。但他就是让其他人替他做这一切。

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:27.450 line:0%
<v Bob>So he would send people to say things to me. I remember one time in the lunchroom Sam saying, have you ever been caught masturbating? And of course, either answer is-- you damn yourself if you answer yes or if you answer no. And I remember hearing Dave tell him to ask me that.

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:27.450 line:80%
<v Bob>他会派人来说那些话。我记得有一次在午餐室，萨姆说，你有没有被抓到过手淫？当然，无论你怎么回答——如果你回答是或不是，你都完了。我记得听到戴夫叫他问我那个问题。

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:59.850 line:0%
<v Bob>I used to just get made fun of horrendously by everyone. People would make fun of the way that I chewed at lunch. People would make fun of the way that I laughed. People would make fun of the way that I coughed, that I sneezed. People would make fun of the way that I walked. There was just this teasing. But that makes it even sound less violent than it was. It was just a mocking. It was a very pointed mocking on everyone's part.

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:59.850 line:80%
<v Bob>我过去常常被所有人极其残忍地嘲笑。人们嘲笑我午饭时咀嚼的方式。人们嘲笑我笑的方式。人们嘲笑我咳嗽、打喷嚏的方式。人们嘲笑我走路的方式。到处都是这种戏弄。但说“戏弄”听起来比实际情况要轻。那是一种赤裸裸的嘲弄。每个人都在进行有针对性的嘲弄。

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:27.960 line:0%
<v Bob>I remember sitting alone at the lunch table with no one to talk to and just wanting so badly for no one to turn their attention to me because I knew that the attention would be negative. I remember not understanding what was going on. I had been in such a sweet position with this person. We had such a good friendship. And then, all of a sudden, everyone had just turned against me.

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:27.960 line:80%
<v Bob>我记得独自坐在午餐桌旁，没有人和我说话，我非常渴望没人把注意力放在我身上，因为我知道那注意力一定是负面的。我不明白发生了什么。我曾和这个人处于那么甜蜜的位置。我们曾有过那么好的友谊。然后，突然之间，所有人都反过来对付我。

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:58.430 line:0%
<v Bob>And he wasn't like a pied piper. He was so subtle, and he was so quiet. I don't think he ever had a friendship with people like he had with me. But he was just able to organize people into these different factions. He would just say, "Go over to [? Cooze ?] and just shove him into a locker." And they'd go do it.

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:58.430 line:80%
<v Bob>他不像吹笛人。他是那么微妙，那么安静。我想他从未像跟我那样跟别人建立过友谊。但他就是能把人们组织成不同的派系。他只会说：“去[？]那里，把他推到储物柜里。”他们就会去照做。

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:15.190 line:0%
<v Bob>That was the worst period, I think, of my entire life was that period. I used to go home from school and cry after school. I used to go to my dog and hug my dog and say, you are my only friend in the world.

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:15.190 line:80%
<v Bob>我认为那是我一生中最糟糕的时期。放学后，我经常回家哭。我曾抱着我的狗说，你是我世界上唯一的朋友。

00:24:15.190 --> 00:24:59.790 line:0%
<v Bob>Then he started to kind of needle me. I remember one thing that he did was there was this guy, Mike, in my class. And Mike was what we called in my hometown a scummer, which meant that he was on welfare. He perhaps didn't have a pair of pants for every day of the week. He was very smart, which was a strike against him at that age. And he just didn't fit in. He was a real geek. So Dave organized this fight between Mike and I-- a fist fight to prove who was the bigger faggot. Whoever would lose was the bigger faggot.

00:24:15.190 --> 00:24:59.790 line:80%
<v Bob>然后他开始折磨我。我记得他做过的一件事，班里有个叫迈克的家伙。迈克在我们家乡被称为“社会渣滓”，意思是他靠救济金生活。他可能一周都没有几条裤子换洗。他非常聪明，但在那个年纪，这反而是他的弱点。他完全不合群。他是个十足的怪胎。所以戴夫策划了我和迈克之间的打斗——一场肉搏战，来证明谁才是更大的娘娘腔。谁输了，谁就是更大的娘娘腔。

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:24.960 line:0%
<v Bob>And he positioned us-- he spun it so there was no way I could not fight. If I didn't have this fight, then things would just get worse for me. Things would get worse. Mike was at the bottom of the barrel, and I was there with him. And Dave had to prove that I was below him. And so he organized this whole fight. And everyone in the class was like, "Are you going to fight Mike? Are you going to fight Mike?"

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:24.960 line:80%
<v Bob>他设了局——他把事情搞得我根本无法拒绝打架。如果不打，情况只会更糟。迈克在社会最底层，我就和他在一起。戴夫必须证明我比他更低。于是他组织了整场打斗。全班都在说：“你要和迈克打吗？你要和迈克打吗？”

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:47.220 line:0%
<v Bob>And I finally conceded. I conceded to fight him. Dave organized the whole event. It was to happen at this playground. And we went, and he tried to get this whole audience to come and see it. Unfortunately, nobody came but him-- well, I guess, fortunately. Nobody came but him and one other person.

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:47.220 line:80%
<v Bob>最后我妥协了。我同意跟他打。戴夫组织了整个活动。地点定在操场上。我们去了，他试图让所有人都来看。不幸的是，没人来，除了他——嗯，我想，幸运的是。没人来，只有他和另外一个人。

00:25:47.220 --> 00:26:15.240 line:0%
<v Bob>So Mike and I went there. And neither of us wanted to fight each other. Neither of us had any reason in the world to fight each other. So I just remember standing there facing him, just laughing, just like, what are we supposed to do? We're standing here. We've been forced into this just to save our own souls. And now we have to inflict pain on each other. And we stood there for the longest time. And Dave kept trying to push us into fighting. He was saying, "Come on. Just throw a punch. Just throw a punch. Just start."

00:25:47.220 --> 00:26:15.240 line:80%
<v Bob>所以迈克和我都去了。我们谁都不想打架。我们根本没有理由打架。我只记得站在那里面对他，大笑着，心想，我们到底该干什么？我们站在这里。我们被强迫这么做，只是为了保全自己的自尊。而现在我们必须伤害彼此。我们站了很久。戴夫一直在催促我们打架。他说：“来吧。挥一拳。挥一拳。开始吧。”

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:41.230 line:0%
<v Bob>We waited for the longest time, and then finally, I was just like, this is ridiculous. So I went up, and I shoved him. And then we started to fight. We weren't really into fighting, but we had to do it then. And I pushed him at one point, and he fell backwards and he hurt his wrist. And I got on top of him. and I held his wrist. And I remember saying to him, "Do you give up?" And he wouldn't give up. And I was holding his wrist back.

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:41.230 line:80%
<v Bob>我们等了很久，最后，我实在受不了了，心想，这太荒谬了。于是我走过去，推了他一把。然后我们就开始打。我们并不是真的想打，但当时不得不这么做。有一次我推他，他向后跌倒，弄伤了手腕。我压在他身上。我抓着他的手腕。我记得对他喊道：“你认输吗？”他不认输。我把他的手腕往后扳。

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:58.740 line:0%
<v Bob>And each time I kept asking if he was going to give up, and he kept saying no. And each time he'd say no, I'd bend his wrist back a little bit more. And he was crying in pain. And I kept saying, I'm going to bend it back. I'm going to bend it back. I'm going to bend it back, thinking, I don't want to break this person's wrist. I have no reason to break this person's wrist.

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:58.740 line:80%
<v Bob>每次我问他是否认输，他都说不。每当他说不，我就把他的手腕再往后扳一点。他在痛苦地哭泣。我一直说，我要把它扳断。我要把它扳断。我要把它扳断。心想，我不想弄断这人的手腕。我没有理由弄断这人的手腕。

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:44.690 line:0%
<v Bob>And I was faced at that moment with either giving up and getting kicked even more or going ahead with really hurting this person and hoping that it would save me in some way. And I broke his wrist. He came in the next day into school with a cast on. I don't know how his family paid for his cast. And everybody saw that and said, "You idiot, [? Cooze! ?] What did you do? You idiot!" And it made it even worse.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:44.690 line:80%
<v Bob>那一刻，我面临两个选择：要么放弃，被踢得更惨；要么继续，真的伤害这个人，希望这能以某种方式救我。我弄断了他的手腕。第二天他打着石膏来上学。我不知道他的家人是怎么支付石膏费用的。每个人都看到了，说：“你这白痴，[？]！你干了什么？你这个白痴！”这让情况变得更糟了。

00:27:44.690 --> 00:28:00.370 line:0%
<v Bob>I just didn't understand. I didn't know what was going on. That's the most frustrating for me, even still today. I always thought it was me. I always thought it was something I was doing. I thought it was the way that I wore my clothes. I thought it was the shoes that I wore. I thought it was the way that I laughed. I remember.

00:27:44.690 --> 00:28:00.370 line:80%
<v Bob>我根本不明白。我不知道发生了什么。即使到今天，那也是最让我沮丧的。我一直认为是我的错。我一直认为是因为我的某些行为。我以为是我的衣着方式。我以为是穿的鞋子。我以为是我笑的方式。我记得。

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:32.790 line:0%
<v Bob>And I remember I tried to change these things. I tried not to laugh so loud. I tried to cough differently. They made fun of the most minuscule things that are just human, like chewing. If they would've noticed the way that I breathed, they would have made fun of that. I remember I tried lifting weights. And it was impossible, because there was nothing I could do to make them change their minds. If I lifted weights, it was the way that I lifted weights. They would come up with something.

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:32.790 line:80%
<v Bob>我记得我试图改变这些。我试着别笑得那么大声。我试着换种方式咳嗽。他们嘲笑最微不足道、最人性化的事，比如咀嚼。如果他们注意到我呼吸的方式，他们也会嘲笑那个的。我记得我试过举重。但这根本不可能，因为我无论怎么做都无法改变他们的想法。如果我举重，他们就会嘲笑我举重的方式。他们总能想出点什么。

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:54.400 line:0%
<v Bob>It was a no-win situation. And I guess I knew how smart he was, and I knew how well he knew me. And I guess, all of a sudden, I had thought that he had seen all these things in me that were just wrong, just so not cool.

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:54.400 line:80%
<v Bob>这是一个必输的局面。我想我知道他有多聪明，也知道他有多了解我。我猜，突然间，我以为他看到了我身上所有那些不对劲、不酷的地方。

00:28:54.400 --> 00:29:20.520 line:0%
<v Bob>I remember very clearly there was a moment that the clouds just cleared and everything became possible. I went to the Pennsylvania Governor's School for the Arts in my junior year. The summer between my junior and senior year, that was when I met people who were actors, like I was, who were my age and who respected me.

00:28:54.400 --> 00:29:20.520 line:80%
<v Bob>我非常清楚地记得，有那么一刻，云开雾散，一切皆有可能。我高三那年去了宾夕法尼亚州州长艺术学校。那是高三和大四之间的那个夏天，我遇到了跟我一样大的演员，他们尊重我。

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:47.410 line:0%
<v Bob>And I remember very clearly, it was after lunch, and people who were in my theater class, we were going down to the cafeteria. And I had forgotten my bag in the cafeteria. And I said, "Oh, just wait for me one second. I just want to get my bag." And I went down to the cafeteria, and I got my bag. And I came back up, and they were still there waiting for me. And I remember so distinctly remembering, "Oh, my god. These people, they waited for me."

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:47.410 line:80%
<v Bob>我记得很清楚，那是午饭后，和我同戏剧班的人要去自助餐厅。我把包忘在餐厅了。我说：“哦，等我一秒钟。我去拿一下包。”我跑下去拿了包，回来时，他们还在等我。我非常清晰地记得那一刻：“天哪。这些人，他们在等我。”

00:29:47.410 --> 00:30:05.590 line:0%
<v Bob>That meant so much to me, because I felt like I'm not this idiot. I'm not this geek. I'm not a person who everyone should hate. I'm actually a pretty good human being, and people like me.

00:29:47.410 --> 00:30:05.590 line:80%
<v Bob>那对我意义重大，因为我感觉我不是那个白痴。我不是那个怪胎。我不是一个每个人都应该讨厌的人。我其实是一个挺好的人，人们喜欢我。

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:24.490 line:0%
<v Bob>Sometimes, I feel like there's some justice in the fact that I'm surrounded now by many people who love me and respect me. And I don't know how many people he has in his life. But the tables have turned in that way.

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:24.490 line:80%
<v Bob>有时，我觉得现在身边围绕着许多爱我、尊重我的人，这本身就是一种正义。我不知道他生活中有多少人。但在那方面，情况已经反转了。

00:30:24.490 --> 00:31:20.850 line:0%
<v Bob>And when I go back home and I see people looking at me the way that they looked at me in high school, I think there is some justice in this, because those people are probably-- some of those people, I've heard, are in bad situations with their families. They've got wives they don't love. They've been through divorces. They don't have any friends. They're in the same bar they were in four or five years ago, drinking the same beer they drank. And their gut is a little bigger, and their nose is a little redder. And they just look tired. And I feel like my life is in pretty good shape, and I'm happy. And that's the sweetest revenge. And last Christmas, I ran into him at a mall. And he looked more jaundiced and more sickly than ever.

00:30:24.490 --> 00:31:20.850 line:80%
<v Bob>当我回到家，看到人们用高中时那样的目光看着我时，我觉得这就是正义，因为那些人——我听说有些人过得很糟。他们有不爱的妻子。他们离过婚。没有任何朋友。他们还在四五年前去的那个酒吧里，喝着同样的啤酒。肚子大了一点，鼻子红了一点。他们看起来只是很累。我觉得我的生活状态很好，我很幸福。那是最甜蜜的复仇。去年圣诞节，我在商场遇到了他。他看起来比以往任何时候都更加面黄肌瘦，更加病态。

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:42.140 line:0%
<v Ira Glass>Bob says that it was because Dave made him an outcast in junior high school that he ended up spending a lot of time with the theater teacher. Because the other kids wouldn't talk to him. And this teacher put him in plays and took him to plays in New York City, showing him that there was a bigger world outside their hometown.

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:42.140 line:80%
<v Ira Glass>鲍勃说，正是因为戴夫在初中时把他变成了排挤对象，他才最终花了很多时间和戏剧老师在一起。因为其他孩子不跟他说话。这位老师让他参加演出，还带他去纽约看戏，让他看到了家乡之外更广阔的世界。

00:31:42.140 --> 00:32:03.800 line:0%
<v Bob>If it weren't for Dave, I would not be doing what I'm doing right now. There's no way. I don't know what I would have been doing, but because of what he did to me, I found theater, and it's been a really good life so far.

00:31:42.140 --> 00:32:03.800 line:80%
<v Bob>如果不是戴夫，我现在绝对不会做我正在做的事。这不可能。我不知道我会做什么，但因为他对我做的一切，我发现了戏剧，到目前为止，生活一直很好。

00:32:03.800 --> 00:33:02.880 line:0%
